{{Politics of Turkey}}

The first round of the '''Presidential elections''' will be held in [[Turkey]] on 10 August 2014,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.reuters.com/article/2014/03/07/us-turkey-election-idUSBREA2610R20140307 |title=Turkey sets Aug 10 for presidential election: source |accessdate=2014-03-21 |publisher=}}</ref> directly for the first time. If no candidate achieves a [[simple majority]], a second round will be held on August 24.

==Background==
The Law on Presidential Elections accepted and put into effect on 20 January 2012 decided that presidential elections will be held in 2014 instead of 2012; within 60 days before the end of the seven-year term of incumbent [[President of Turkey]] [[Abdullah Gül]], who will be the last indirectly elected [[President of Turkey]]. As Gül took office on August 28, 2007, the first possible day could be June 29, but a date between August 10 and August 17 is considered more reasonable.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.turkiyegazetesi.com.tr/gundem/123967.aspx |language=Turkish |title=Cumhurbaşkanı seçimi ağustos başı yapılacak |accessdate=2014-03-05 |publisher=}}</ref>

It was decided in the same law that former Presidents [[Kenan Evren]], [[Süleyman Demirel]], [[Ahmet Necdet Sezer]] and incumbent [[Abdullah Gül]] cannot be nominated for a second term, but this was ruled unconstitutional by the [[Constitutional Court of Turkey]], allowing them to run for a second term. Also, the [[Prime Minister of Turkey]] can be nominated for the presidency without having to resign from his post.

The presidential elections campaign will be held in an "American manner", where candidates can accept personal donations for their campaigns; however, one person cannot donate more than 8,259 TL (approximately $4,500 USD) to a campaign.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://siyaset.milliyet.com.tr/cankaya-yasasi-meclis-ten-gecti/siyaset/siyasetdetay/20.01.2012/1491396/default.htm |language=Turkish |title=Çankaya yasası Meclis’ten geçti |accessdate=2012-09-02 |publisher=}}</ref>

==Official candidates==
* [[Recep Tayyip Erdoğan]], incumbent [[Prime Minister of Turkey]] <ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.todayszaman.com/news-350260-pm-as-candidate-for-president-may-receive-half-of-overseas-vote.html|title=PM as candidate for president may receive half of overseas vote|work=[[Today's Zaman]]|date=14 June 2014|accessdate=21 June 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.aa.com.tr/en/news/322653--turkeys-deputy-pm-backs-erdogan-for-president|title=Turkey's Deputy PM backs Erdogan for president|work=[[Anadolu Agency]]|date=4 May 2014|accessdate=21 June 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Çevik |first=İlnur |date=2014-05-10 |title=Can the opposition have any change against Erdogan?|url=http://www.dailysabah.com/columns/ilnur-cevik/2014/05/10/does-the-opposition-have-any-chance-against-erdogan |newspaper=Daily Sabah|location=Balmumcu, Istanbul |publisher=[[Sabah (newspaper)|Sabah]] |accessdate=2014-06-21}}</ref>
* [[Ekmeleddin İhsanoğlu]], former Secretary-General of the [[Organisation of Islamic Cooperation]] (Nominated by [[Republican People's Party (Turkey)|CHP]] and [[Nationalist Movement Party|MHP]],<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.ft.com/intl/cms/s/0/4a9a34d2-f570-11e3-be21-00144feabdc0.html#axzz35H7qgjn9|title=Turkey’s opposition choose joint presidential candidate|work=[[Financial Times]]|date=16 June 2014|accessdate=21 June 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.hurriyetdailynews.com/turkeys-main-opposition-parties-to-nominate-ex-head-of-islamic-body-ihsanoglu-for-presidency.aspx?pageID=238&nID=67850&NewsCatID=338|title=Turkey’s main opposition parties to nominate ex-head of Islamic body İhsanoğlu for presidency|work=[[Hürriyet Daily News]]|date=16 June 2014|accessdate=21 June 2014}}</ref> <br/ > Supported by [[Democratic Party (Turkey, current)|DP]], [[Democratic Left Party (Turkey)|DSP]], [[Independent Turkey Party|BTP]],<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.ntvmsnbc.com/id/25524244|title=5 partiden ‘çatı aday’a destek|work=[[NTV (Turkey)|NTV]]|date=2 July 2014|accessdate=2 July 2014}}</ref> [[Liberal Democratic Party (Turkey)|LDP]] <ref>{{cite news|url=http://188.95.146.137/news/LDP-Ihsanoglu-na-destek-karari-aldi_1407-CHMTQ5MTQwNy8xMDA1|title=LDP, İhsanoğlu'na destek kararı aldı|work=[[Cihan News Agency]]|date=1 July 2014|accessdate=7 July 2014}}</ref> and [[Great Union Party|BBP]] <ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.aa.com.tr/tr/haberler/359575--bbp-ihsanoglunu-destekleme-karari-aldi|title=BBP İhsanoğlu'nu destekleme kararı aldı|work=[[Anadolu News Agency]]|date=16 July 2014|accessdate=16 July 2014}}</ref>)
* [[Selahattin Demirtaş]], leader of [[Peoples' Democratic Party (Turkey)|Peoples' Democratic Party]] <ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.cnnturk.com/haber/turkiye/selahattin-demirtas-hdpnin-cumhurbaskani-adayi-mi|title=Selahattin Demirtaş HDP'nin cumhurbaşkanı adayı mı?|work=[[CNN Turk]]|date=18 June 2014|accessdate=21 June 2014}}</ref>

==Campaigns==

The campaigns of all three candidates centred mainly on the [[Israel–Gaza conflict|2014 Israel–Gaza conflict]], the peace process with [[PKK]] rebels, the [[Gezi Park protests]] and the 17 December 2013 [[2013 corruption scandal in Turkey|government corruption scandal]]

===Erdoğan campaign===

Incumbent Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdoğan formally announced his candidacy for the Presidency on 1 July 2014 in a speech that lasted just over an hour.<ref>{{cite news|title=AK Parti'nin cumhurbaşkanı adayı Recep Tayyip Erdoğan|url=http://www.cnnturk.com/haber/turkiye/ak-partinin-cumhurbaskani-adayi-recep-tayyip-erdogan|accessdate=3 August 2014|work=CNN Türk|agency=01 July 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=AK Parti'nin cumhurbaşkanı adayı Recep Tayyip Erdoğan|url=http://www.hurriyet.com.tr/gundem/26719347.asp|accessdate=3 August 2014|work=Hürriyet|agency=01 July 2014}}</ref> Speculation that he would be the AK Party's candidate was widespread long before the announcement.<ref>{{cite news|title=Erdoşan's candidacy for presidency to be announced 1st July|url=http://en.protothema.gr/turkey-erdogans-candidacy-for-president-to-be-announced-on-1st-of-july/|accessdate=3 August 2014|work=Protothema News|agency=24 June 2014}}</ref><ref>http://www.dailystar.com.lb/News/Middle-East/2014/Jun-22/261109-turkey-pm-erdogan-says-ak-party-is-not-one-mans-party</ref> On the same day, the Erdoğan logo was unveiled, and received criticism for bearing resemblance to the [[Barack Obama|Obama]] [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008 presidential election]] campaign logo, as well as the ''Holiday Place'' logo.<ref>http://www.huffingtonpost.com/alparslan-akku/erdogans-presidential-cam_b_5567633.html</ref> A tweet from the [[Ministry of Finance (Turkey)|finance minister]], [[Mehmet Şimşek]], which claimed that the logo has [[Islamic calligraphy|Arabic calligraphy]] of the [[Muhammad|Prophet Muhammad's]] name also sparked debate over the use of religion in the political campaign.<ref>http://www.todayszaman.com/news-352346-erdogans-logo-ignites-debate-over-political-abuses-of-religion.html</ref> Erdoğan's campaign slogan is "''Milli İrade, Milli Güç, Hedef 2023''" (''National Will, National Strength, Target 2023''). On 11 July, Erdoğan unveiled a new campaign song and slogan, "''Yeni Türkiye Yolunda Demokrasi, Refah, İtibar''" (''Democracy, Prosperity and Prestige on the Road to a New Turkey'').<ref>http://www.internethaber.com/iste-erdoganin-secim-slogani-696355h.htm</ref>

Erdoğan's campaign has been dominated by electoral rallies, beginning in [[Samsun]] on 5 July and then moving to [[Erzurum]], mimicking [[Mustafa Kemal Atatürk|Mustafa Kemal Atatürk's]] route which he took at the start of the [[Turkish War of Independence]].<ref>http://www.beyazgazete.com/video/webtv/siyaset-3/cumhurbaskani-adayi-ve-basbakan-erdogan-in-samsun-mitingi-427047.html</ref> His rally speeches mainly centred on his achievements as Prime Minister and also contained frequent attacks on both the opposition as well as [[Fethullah Gülen]], the leader of the [[Hizmet]] Movement living in [[Pennsylvania]]. During his [[Hatay]] rally on 21 July, he accused Gülen of not speaking out on behalf of the [[Palestinian people|Palestinians]] in [[Gaza]] and accused [[Israel]] of "following in the footsteps of [[Hitler]]".<ref>http://www.ajanskonya.net/siyaset/recep-tayyip-erdogan-hatay-mitingi-konusmasinda-h48196.html</ref><ref>http://www.ensonhaber.com/basbakan-erdoganin-hatay-mitingi-konusmasi-2014-03-22.html</ref> During his [[Kahramanmaraş]] rally on 1 August, he claimed that the opposition CHP was supporting Israel during the Gaza crisis.<ref>http://www.aa.com.tr/tr/politika/367381--basbakan-erdogan-kahramanmarasta</ref>

During his electoral rally in [[Van (Turkey)|Van]], Erdoğan attacked his rival Ekmeleddin İhsanoğlu for allegedly mistaking the [[Independence March]], the Turkish [[National Anthem]], for a poem composed for the fallen soldiers at [[Çanakkale]] during the [[Gallipoli Campaign]]. In his [[Kahramanmaraş]] rally on 1 August, he showed the crowd a video of İhsanoğlu misreading the Independence March.<ref>http://www.milliyet.com.tr/basbakan-kahramanmaras-ta-konusuyor/siyaset/detay/1919657/default.htm</ref>

In addition to his electoral rallies, AK Party activists have also launched a door-to-door operation in order to gather support by delivering food, clothing and other items to families. According to the [[OSCE]], the Erdoğan campaign has also organised [[iftar]] tents during the month of [[Ramadan]] and has distributed toys and women's scarves at electoral rallies.<ref name="hurriyetdailynews.com">http://www.hurriyetdailynews.com/osce-slams-use-of-public-funds-for-turkish-pm-erdogans-presidential-campaign.aspx?pageID=238&nID=69880&NewsCatID=338</ref> On 15 July 2014, the government began a huge distribution of free coal to families in [[İzmir]].<ref>http://gundem.bugun.com.tr/secim-oncesi-bedava-komur-dagitimi-haberi/1187562</ref>

===İhsanoğlu campaign===

[[Ekmeleddin İhsanoğlu]] was announced as the joint candidate of the [[Republican People's Party (Turkey)|Republican People's Party]] (CHP) and the [[Nationalist Movement Party]] (MHP) on 16 June 2014.<ref>http://www.haberturk.com/polemik/haber/958500-chp-ve-mhpnin-cati-adayi-belli-oldu-ekmeleddin-ihsanoglu-kimdir</ref><ref>http://www.sabah.com.tr/Gundem/2014/06/16/iste-muhalefetin-kosk-adayi</ref> The decision drew criticism from many CHP supporters due to his former role as Secretary General of the [[Organisation of Islamic Cooperation]], which some members of the CHP saw as an organisation which lacked secular and [[Kemalist]] credentials.<ref>http://www.radikal.com.tr/politika/ekmeleddin_ihsanoglu_ataturk_ve_cumhuriyet_dusmani_mi-1197651</ref> An opinion poll on 23 June showed that only 32% of CHP supporters were happy with their party's choice of candidate, compared to 75% of MHP supporters. Meanwhile, 68% of CHP supporters were dissatisfied with the decision, compared to only 25% of MHP supporters.<ref>http://www.ilk-kursun.com/haber/186271/cati-adayli-ilk-secim-anketi/</ref> In response to the controversy over his secular credentials, İhsanoğlu stated on 26 June that he thought of [[Mustafa Kemal Atatürk]] as a "national hero" and that he had defended secularism in many of his books.<ref>http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QJDNG-CNCrg</ref> On 4 July, he stated that he is "not an enemy of Atatürk," and that "incorporating religion into politics will lead to trouble."<ref>http://www.haber7.com/roportaj/haber/1176363-ekmeleddin-ihsanoglu-laikligi-savunuyorum</ref> Former CHP leader [[Deniz Baykal]], who was seen as a potential CHP nominee, criticised İhsanoğlu's candidacy, stating that the CHP and MHP should have held a vote amongst their members to chose a candidate.<ref>http://www.egedesonsoz.com/haber/Baykal-dan-cok-sert-cati-aday-aciklamalari/872174</ref><ref>http://www.erkenhaberal.com/baykaldancati-aday-icin-ilk-tepki/</ref>

On 7 July, İhsanoğlu stated that he would not base his campaign on election rallies, stating, "how right is it to force people outside under the heat in this holy month of [[Ramadan]]? When are we going to hold the rallies, before [[sahur]] or after [[iftar]]? We are visiting our provinces and districts, meeting local leaders and visiting our people." He also expressed his wish to hold a live televised debate with Erdoğan.<ref>http://www.gercekgundem.com/siyaset/55278/iste-ihsanoglunun-miting-yapmama-nedeni</ref><ref>http://www.samanyoluhaber.com/politika/Ekmeleddin-Ihsanoglu-neden-miting-yapmadigini-acikladi/1056479/</ref><ref>http://www.avrupagazete.com/mobile/turkiye/92903-ekmeleddin-ihsanoglu-nun-miting-yapmama-nedeni.html</ref> Following the conclusion of Ramadan, İhsanoğlu held his first election rally in [[Hatay]] on 2 August, reiterating his support for state secularism.<ref>http://videonuz.ensonhaber.com/izle/ekmeleddin-ihsanoglu-ilk-kez-hatay-da-miting-yapti</ref><ref>http://www.ensonhaber.com/ekmeleddin-ihsanoglu-ilk-kez-hatayda-miting-yapti-2014-08-02.html</ref> İhsanoğlu's campaign slogan is "''Ekmek için Ekmeleddin''" (''Ekmeleddin for bread''), other slogans which will be used for the campaign include "Ekmeleddin to cultivate love, to cultivate prosperity and for bread." "Ekmek" means "to cultivate" at the same time.<ref>http://www.dailysabah.com/politics/2014/07/10/ihsanoglu-announces-his-election-campaign-program</ref>

Following Erdoğan's accusation that İhsanoğlu did not know the [[Independence March]], İhsanoğlu responded by saying that since the author of the March, [[Mehmet Akif Ersoy]] was the best friend of his father, he had already learnt the anthem as a baby.<ref>http://www.zaman.com.tr/politika_ekmeleddin-ihsanoglu-istiklal-marsi-mevzuu-yanlis-yansitildi_2235160.html</ref> In a statement, he claimed that the video that Erdoğan showed during his [[Kahramanmaraş]] rally was "doctored," and that he had "translated this unrivalled piece into [[Arabic]], how is it possible that I wouldn't know it?" <ref>http://www.cumhuriyet.com.tr/haber/turkiye/100717/ihsanoglu_ndan_istiklal_Marsi_aciklamasi.html</ref> On 2 August, the pro-opposition newspaper [[Sözcü]] published footage of Recep Tayyip Erdoğan himself misreading the Independence March.<ref>http://sozcu.com.tr/2014/gundem/erdoganda-istiklal-marsini-yanlis-okumus-568963/</ref>

On 16 July, İhsanoğlu visited the Palestinian embassy in [[Ankara]], denying reports that he had said on 4 July that [[Turkey]] should remain "neutral" in in the Israeli-Palestinian issue.<ref>http://www.haber7.com/televizyon/haber/1176287-ihsanoglundan-sok-filistin-ve-israil-yorumu</ref><ref>http://gundem.bugun.com.tr/noktayi-boyle-koydu-haberi/1187558</ref> In a statement, he said that "I have never advocated Turkish neutrality. You cannot be neutral between the cruel and the oppressed."<ref>http://hergunmedya.com/haber/ekmeleddin-ihsanoglu-filistin-devleti-buyukelciligini-ziyaret-etti/6439/</ref> On 19 July, he stated that Palestine should take their case to the [[International Criminal Court]].<ref>http://www.haberturk.com/gundem/haber/971519-ihsanoglu-filistin-uluslararasi-ceza-mahkemesine-gitmeli</ref>

In his vision, İhsanoğlu vowed to protect democracy, the rule of law, the separation of powers, the principles of Atatürk and national unity. He also advocated waiving the debts of Turkey's poorest citizens. He further stressed the need for greater involvement of non-governmental organisations as well as local authorities in the decision making process in order to strengthen democracy. In addition, he stresses the importance of environmental protection, greater rights to women and educational assistance to the nation's poorest children in order to turn Turkey into a "nation of knowledge" such as [[South Korea]]. İhsanoğlu states that because he is not the candidate of a single party, he would be able to solve national tensions between the opposition and government as well as bring about peace and stability to both Turkey and to the world through foreign policy.<ref>http://ihsanoglu.com/vizyon/</ref>

===Demirtaş campaign===

Selahattin Demirtaş was announced as a candidate for the Presidency on 30 June.<ref>http://www.ntvmsnbc.com/id/25523738/</ref> In a campaign dominated by the "peace process" with Kurdish rebels, he claimed on 5 August in [[Van (Turkey)|Van]] that the government of Recep Tayyip Erdoğan had not done enough to bring forward promised legislation, and that the process would collapse immediately if the AKP did not do more to bring lasting peace in the southeast.<ref>http://www.milliyet.com.tr/demirtas-baris-surecinin/siyaset/detay/1921365/default.htm</ref> He is the co-leader of the [[Peoples' Democratic Party (Turkey)|Peoples' Democratic Party]] (HDP), serving alongside [[Figen Yüksekdağ]].

On 15 July, Demirtaş outlined his road-map for his presidency should he win the election. In a speech lasting just under an hour, he proposed that the [[Ministry of Religious Affairs]] (Diyanet) should be disbanded, that compulsory religion lessons in schools should be removed and that [[Cemevi|Cemevis]] (the [[Alevi]] houses of worship) should receive national recognition.<ref>http://www.yurtgazetesi.com.tr/politika/diyanet-kaldirilmali-cemevleri-yasal-statuye-kavusturulmali-h56640.html</ref> He also proposed the introduction of "People's Parliaments" ''(Cumhur meclisleri)'', which would also incorporate Youth Parliaments to increase representation of young citizens.<ref>http://sozcu.com.tr/2014/gundem/demirtastan-turk-bayragi-mesaji-556375/</ref> Pushing for a new constitution, Demirtaş outlined the need to end the non-representation of different cultures, languages, races and beliefs without delay to ensure national stability.<ref>http://www.wanhaber.com/selahattin-demirtas-yol-haritasini-acikladi-193637h.htm</ref> Also in his speech, he praised the [[Gezi Park protests]] and displayed photos of himself during the events. He continued to direct applause to the mother of the murdered teenager [[Berkin Elvan]], who died 269 days after being hit by a tear gas canister during the protests and falling into a coma.<ref>http://www.dunya.com/demirtas-kosk-icin-yol-haritasini-acikladi-233457h.htm</ref> On the issue of the lack of Turkish flags within the hall in which he was delivering his speech, Demirtaş stated that the Turkish flag represented all citizens of Turkey.<ref>http://www.turkiyegazetesi.com.tr/politika/171293.aspx</ref> His slogan is ''"Bir Cumhurbaşkanı Düşün" (Imagine a President...)'', which is followed by several different phrases, such as ''"Bir Cumhurbaşkanı Düşünün Ayrımcılık yapmıyor. Birleştiriyor, barıştırıyor." (Imagine a President who doesn't Discriminate, who Unites and makes Peace)'' or ''"Bir Cumhurbaşkanı Düşünün Herkese Demokrat" (Imagine a President who is Democratic to Everybody).''<ref>http://www.yurtgazetesi.com.tr/politika/diyanet-kaldirilmali-cemevleri-yasal-statuye-kavusturulmali-h56640.html</ref>

On 18 July, Demirtaş visited [[Paris]], [[France]], and laid flowers at the scene where three Kurdish activists were shot dead on 9 January 2013.<ref>http://t24.com.tr/haber/selahattin-demirtas-pariste-3-pkklinin-olduruldugu-yere-cicek-birakti,264833</ref>

