==Controversies==

===Pre-election===

Upon the announcement of Recep Tayyip Erdoğan's candidacy, the [[Republican People's Party (Turkey)|CHP]], [[Liberal Democratic Party (Turkey)|LDP]] and [[Nationalist Movement Party|MHP]] contested his refusal to resign as Prime Minister during the campaign. The case was taken to the [[Supreme Electoral Council of Turkey|Supreme Electoral Council]] by both the MHP and [[Mahmut Tanal]], a CHP MP from [[İstanbul]] on 9 July.<ref>http://www.turkiyegazetesi.com.tr/politika/169774.aspx</ref> In a press statement, Tanal also attributed the case to the alleged incompatibility of Erdoğan's ideologies and the history of his premiership to the office of the Presidency, and referred to the 2008 judicial verdict which curbed the AKP's state funding due to the "violations of democratic and secular principles."<ref>http://gundem.bugun.com.tr/chp-ve-mhp-itiraz-etti-haberi/1179529</ref><ref>http://www.ajanshaber.com/basbakan-erdoganin-adayligina-itiraz-haberi/91302</ref> The [[Constitutional Court of Turkey|Constitutional Court]] affirmed Erdoğan's eligibility to remain as Prime Minister while standing as a presidential candidate on 24 July.<ref>http://www.dunyabulteni.net/erdoganin-adayligina-itiraz-reddedildi/304785/erdoganin-adayligina-itiraz-reddedildi</ref>

On 1 August, several online newspapers reported that the [[Anadolu Agency|Anatolian Agency]] had used what seemed to be pre-determined election results while doing a practice run for their election night coverage. Leaked images of teleprompter text allegedly showed the Anatolian Agency using pre-determined election turnouts and results which placed Recep Tayyip Erdoğan firmly in first place in six provinces, with 73% in [[Bartın]], 82% in [[Rize]], 80% in [[Gümüşhane]], 70% in [[Kahramanmaraş]], 75% in [[Konya]] and 80% in [[Isparta]]. However, the authenticity of the leaked images have not been verified and newspapers which reported the news did not name a source.<ref>http://www.ulusalkanal.com.tr/gundem/anadolu-ajansi-secim-sonuclarini-acikladi-h33596.html</ref><ref>http://www.muhalifgazete.com/haber/114382/anadolu-ajansi-secim-sonuclarini-simdiden-acikladi.html</ref><ref>http://www.ajans32.com/anadolu-ajansi-secim-sonuclarini-simdiden-acikladi-23409h.htm</ref><ref>http://www.odatv.com/n.php?n=anadolu-ajanssi-secim-sonuclarini-simdiden-acikladi-3107141200</ref><ref>http://www.muhalifbaski.com/anadolu-ajansi-secim-sonuclarini-simdiden-acikladi-15237h.htm</ref>

In a report released by the [[Office for Democratic Institutions and Human Rights]] (ODIHR) and the [[Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe]] (OSCE) on 31 July, Erdoğan was criticised for the alleged use of public funds to finance his campaign. In addition to the distribution of free coal in [[İzmir]],<ref>http://www.yuksekovahaber.com/haber/izmirde-komur-yardimi-tartismasi-134423.htm</ref> the OSCE also noted that the campaign had begun an excessive distribution of food, clothing, toys, cups, mouse mats and scarves both at election rallies and during door-to-door canvassing.<ref>http://www.turkishweekly.net/news/169773/osce-worried-over-fairness-of-turkey-39-s-presidential-elections.html</ref> The OSCE also drew attention to the fact that Erdoğan used the opening of the [[Ankara-Istanbul high-speed railway]] on 25 July to promote his candidacy, and have criticised the "limited public visibility of the other candidates" in contrast to Erdoğan's huge and widely broadcast electoral rallies.<ref>http://en.cihan.com.tr/news/OSCE-criticizes-unfair-competition-pressure-on-media-in-report_4036-CHMTUxNDAzNi80</ref> Both the rival candidates and the OSCE have called for greater transparency regarding the financing of Erdoğan's campaign, and the latter has decided to send a delegation to monitor the elections on 10 August.<ref name="hurriyetdailynews.com"/><ref>http://www.todayszaman.com/news-354237-osce-to-observe-presidential-elections-in-turkey.html</ref>

Further accusation over the use of public resources in Erdoğan's campaign have been fueled by opposition journalists, who have accused Erdoğan of using a fake license plates to disguise his use of an official car during his campaign.<ref>http://www.cumhuriyet.com.tr/haber/turkiye/100483/Erdogan_in_Mercedes_inin_plakasi_Fiat_a_ait_cikti.html</ref> Journalists have also drawn attention to the alleged use of official helicopters and planes by the Prime Minister to fly between election rally destinations, despite the requirement by law which restricts the use of such transport for official duties only.<ref>http://sozcu.com.tr/2014/gundem/erdogan-sahte-plakayi-degistirdi-568678/</ref> Following the accusations, a CHP MP from İstanbul, [[Umut Oran]], brought claims of the misuse of public resources before the Supreme Electoral Council and requested an audit into donations to Erdoğan's campaign.<ref>http://en.cihan.com.tr/news/Erdogan-uses-fake-license-plate-to-avoid-election-ban-daily-says_3974-CHMTUxMzk3NC8xMDA1</ref> In response to the claims that Erdoğan used fake license plates, the Turkish Prime Ministry issued a statement on 1 August stating that all four cars used by Erdoğan during the campaign had been rented, and that one license plate (06 BV 8534) which had been accused of being fake had been accidently misprinted and was in fact 06 BV 8543.<ref>http://www.sabah.com.tr/Gundem/2014/08/01/basbakanliktan-sahte-plaka-aciklamasi</ref>

On 6 August, it emerged that the [[Ministry of National Education (Turkey)|Ministry of National Education]] (MEB) had printed an exam revision guide which included Recep Tayyip Erdoğan in the list of Turkish Presidents four days before the election took place. The leader of the guide's distributor, Ahmet Gündoğdu, stated that "this is a known outcome. Even if he doesn't win on the first round, Recep Tayyip Erdoğan will definitely win on the second. If he doesn't, we'll only have misprinted the questions - but he will."<ref>http://www.haberturk.com/gundem/haber/977095-recep-tayyip-erdogani-12-cumhurbaskani-olarak-yazdilar</ref> On the same day, the MEB issued a formal statement which denied the claims that they had assumed the election result before it had taken place, stating that the book in question was in fact a book about Atatürk's principles and history, and that no changes had been made to it since 2008.<ref>http://www.cnnturk.com/haber/turkiye/meb-erdogani-cumhurbaskani-yapti-iddialarina-yanit</ref>

On 8 August, a voter from [[Didim]], [[Aydın]] claimed that four unknown voters were recorded to have been living in his household according to the Supreme Electoral Council.<ref>http://www.ulkucumedya.com/secim-hileleri-basladi.html</ref> The lack of up-to-date voter data, which listed dead persons as voters as well as addresses that did not exist had previously resulted in huge controversy in the [[Turkish local elections, 2014|2014 local elections]].

Controversy surrounded the role and use of the [[Turkish flag]] by the candidates for their election campaigns. The Supreme Electoral Council had controversially regulated its use previously for the local elections.<ref>http://www.hurriyet.com.tr/gundem/25283330.asp</ref> In İhsanoğlu's earlier election adverts, subtitles notified viewers that they had been banned from including the Turkish flag in the advert by the Electoral Council.

The use of religion in the campaigns also fuelled controversy. Erdoğan's campaign logo, which had already generated controversy due to its resemblance of [[Barack Obama|Barack Obama's]] [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008 presidential campaign]] logo, had been claimed to have [[Islamic calligraphy|Arabic calligraphy]] of the [[Muhammad|Prophet Muhammad's]] name by the [[Ministry of Finance (Turkey)|finance minister]] [[Mehmet Şimşek]].<ref>http://www.todayszaman.com/news-352346-erdogans-logo-ignites-debate-over-political-abuses-of-religion.html</ref> On 5 August, the Supreme Electoral Council banned an Erdoğan campaign advertisement on the grounds that it "abused religious feelings by depicting the [[Azan]], a prayer mat and a woman performing [[Namaz]]."<ref>http://www.milliyet.com.tr/ysk-erdogan-in-reklam-filmini/siyaset/detay/1921327/default.htm</ref>

On 7 August, it was reported that security forces prevented citizens attending Erdoğan's [[Gaziantep]] rally from leaving early. One person was detained at the scene.<ref>http://sozcu.com.tr/2014/gundem/erdoganin-mitinginden-ayrilmak-isteyen-vatandaslara-engel-573052/</ref>

